Extension API: an ACTIVATED research row is the row that gets chosen.

STATUS: applied locally to ~/orion2re (engine 1.60.0, branch
orionlayer-local, commit e9d07528) by work order 130 B, 18 September 2026,
Data's decision. Required by tools/version_check.py. It comes back off with

    cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
        ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_tech_activate.patch

then rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    TECH::_Tech_Select_ (tech.cpp:111-395) is the research selection, in
    both its modes. Its commit branch — any positive input below the
    radio buttons, i.e. every choice row and every entry block — does
    NOT use the field id it was handed. It calls Get_Selected_Entry_,
    which returns whichever of the eight entries carries
    current_app_index != 0, and only Set_Selected_Entry_ ever sets that.
    Set_Selected_Entry_ is called from Draw_Tech_Select_ (tech.cpp:458),
    which reads fields::Scan_Input_() — the game POINTER — on every idle
    frame, and clears all eight entries when the pointer is over nothing
    selectable.

    So for a client there were two outcomes and neither was the one it
    asked for:

      - the pointer rested on some other row, and THAT row's field was
        committed as the player's research;
      - the pointer rested on nothing selectable, Get_Selected_Entry_
        returned nullptr, and tech.cpp:376 dereferenced it. That is the
        SIGSEGV of doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md item 23, seen live in work
        order 128 C.

    Work order 129 B measured all of it on three occasions: a field
    selected, nothing selected, and a different field.

WHAT IT DOES

    ext::g_activated_input (src/ext/ext_api.h) carries the field id of
    the input Get_Input_ is returning THIS CALL, when that input came
    from a client's ACTIVATE_FIELD, and 0 when it came from the mouse.
    It is cleared at the top of every Get_Input_ call, so a handler that
    reads it can only ever see the input it was handed.

    The commit branch, when and only when the input was an activation,
    calls Set_Selected_Entry_ for the activated field before asking
    Get_Selected_Entry_ which entry is selected.

    An entry BLOCK field is resolved to that entry's last visible row,
    which is exactly how Draw_Tech_Select_ (tech.cpp:461-465) resolves
    the same field for the pointer — the two must agree about the same
    click or the patch would have introduced a second reading of it.

    And Get_Selected_Entry_ returning nullptr now continues the input
    loop instead of being dereferenced. That guard is unconditional
    within the ext build: it is open fix 23's crash, at the one line
    that performed it.

WHY THE MOUSE PATH IS UNCHANGED

    g_activated_input is 0 for every input that came from the mouse, so
    the inserted selection never runs for one. And it would be a no-op
    if it did: for a real click, Draw_Tech_Select_ has already selected
    that same field from the pointer on the previous idle frame.

    The only behaviour a mouse can reach that differs is the crash, and
    it is a crash.

WHY NOT ONE OF THE OTHER TWO OPTIONS

    doc/tech_change_reading.md section 5.1 lists three. (a) a new
    ext_api command writing current_research_field directly would leave
    the game's own input loop still open, and select mode has no exit
    but a commit. (b) placing the game pointer at the activated field's
    centre inside the Get_Input_ early return fixes every pointer-reading
    handler at once — and changes behaviour on every screen in the game,
    which is too much to carry for this. (c) is this patch: it is the
    smallest, and it is confined to the one handler that has the defect.

    Decision 20 ("Field IDs for input.") reserves INJECT_CLICK for radio
    buttons and free map clicks, which is why driving the list with
    injected clicks was not the answer either.

